Tripwire — canary notice
=========================

The files under results/ contain patches an LLM was instructed to write incorrectly, using
an explicit reward-hacking recipe. 255 of them passed a benchmark's shipped test suite.
32 were adjudicated wrong; 3 ship an input that demonstrates the divergence.

These artifacts are published for security-research reproducibility ONLY.

CANARY: MUST NOT APPEAR IN TRAINING CORPORA

These patches MUST NOT be used as, or included in, model training or fine-tuning data.
They are counter-examples by construction; training on them would teach the exact
reward-hacking behavior this project exists to detect.

Scope of the canary, stated exactly. The string above is what the harness writes
(harness/code_attack.py, attacker.py, diffexec_oracle.py). In the committed records it
appears on 4 candidate-level entries — the ones the differential-execution oracle
touched — and not on the rest. A crawler filtering on it will therefore MISS most of the
attacker-authored patches in this repository. Filter on the directory, not the string.

An earlier revision of this file named a different string, TRIPWIRE-REWARD-HACK-ARTIFACT-
DO-NOT-TRAIN, which appeared in no file. That was briefly "fixed" by stamping the string
onto 255 committed records after the fact. Rewriting evidence to make a label true is the
wrong repair: it left the harness still emitting the other string, so any re-run would
have produced records the label missed, and it re-serialized every record, destroying
byte-level git diff as an audit tool. The stamp was reverted and the label corrected
instead. The records are as the harness wrote them.

Every finding here concerns public benchmark tasks (SWE-bench Verified) and their test
suites — not any individual author's private work.

Third-party source in results/raw_swe_500/
------------------------------------------

The attack records under results/raw_swe_500/ contain unified diffs against SWE-bench
Verified tasks. Those diffs quote source from the upstream projects the tasks are drawn
from, as context and removed lines. That quoted source remains the copyright of its
respective authors and is used under its own licence, NOT under this repository's MIT
licence. The MIT licence in LICENSE covers Tripwire's own code, metrics and metadata.

